@meta
id: 09-19
title: Scenario — legal say stop the email
chapter: 09
type: scenario
skills: op-motion, op-linewise, op-counts, op-to-mark
par: 44
minutes: 10
vscode: two tabs, four careful selections, one file you must not save
open: customer.provider.ts customer.entity.ts notification.service.ts

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    CUS-77   We are sending a marketing email every time a customer
             edits their own profile. Legal want it gone today. While
             you are in there, the CSV exporter went with the admin
             tool last sprint and the notification gateway no longer
             takes an email address.

Three files, already open as three buffers. `:bn` and `:bp` move between
them, `:ls` lists them.

  1. customer.provider.ts

     - The `sendMarketing` call in `update` goes. Nothing replaces it.
     - The `notify` call must stop passing `customer.email`. The first
       two arguments and the closing bracket stay exactly as they are.
     - `exportToCsv` is dead with the admin tool. The method goes, and
       one of the blank lines around it, leaving the single blank line
       that separates every other method in the file.

  2. customer.entity.ts — `lastExportedAt` was only ever written by the
     exporter. Remove it. `marketingOptIn` stays; other services read it.

  3. notification.service.ts — nothing to do. It is open so you can check
     that `notify` really does take two arguments now. Change it and the
     check fails.

@keys
| `dt)` | delete up to, not including, the closing bracket |
| `d}` | delete from here to the next blank line |
| `2f,` | onto the second comma on this line |
| `:bn` `:bp` `:ls` | next buffer, previous buffer, list them |
| `:w` | write — vim will not leave a modified buffer without it |
| `:wqa` | write every buffer and quit |

@hints
Work bottom-up within the provider. Every line you delete moves the lines
below it, so if you start at the top you will be re-reading line numbers
after every edit.
--
None of the four edits wants a count. The argument is bounded by a
bracket, the stale call is one line, the dead method is a paragraph, and
the entity field is one line.
--
In the provider: line 29 is the `notify` call, `2f,` then `dt)`. Line 28
is the marketing call, `dd`. Line 20 is the blank line above
`exportToCsv`, `d}`. Then `:w`, `:bn`, and the entity field is `5G dd`.

@solution
A reasonable route, buffer by buffer.

**customer.provider.ts** — bottom-up:

    29G 2f, dt)     drop the third argument, keep the bracket
    28G dd          drop the marketing call
    20G d}          drop the dead method and one blank line
    :w

**customer.entity.ts** — `:bn`, then `5G dd`, then `:wqa` to write
everything and leave.

**notification.service.ts** — do not touch it.

Around thirty-five keystrokes for a four-part change across two files,
and not one of them was a selection.

Three things in here are worth taking away.

The provider's three edits are three different extents — a bracket, a
line, a paragraph — and each one has a motion that describes it exactly.
The alternative, counting, would have worked and would have been wrong
the next time somebody adds a line to `update`.

Bottom-up is not fussiness. `d}` on line 20 shifts everything below it by
four lines; do that first and the `notify` call is no longer on line 29,
and you will not notice until you have deleted the wrong thing.

And the decoy file is the real test. Vim makes it very easy to wander
into a buffer, press something, and write it without meaning to. `:wqa`
writes all three files here, which is fine precisely because you left the
third one alone. If you are not sure whether you touched it, `:ls` marks
modified buffers with a `+`.

@start customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';
import { NotificationService } from '../../infrastructure/services/notification.service';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(NotificationService) protected notificationService: NotificationService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }
    return customer;
  }

  public async exportToCsv(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(id);
    return [customer.id, customer.email, customer.name].join(',');
  }

  public async update(id: string, values: Partial<Customer>) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(id);
    await this.notificationService.sendMarketing(customer.email, 'PROFILE_UPDATED');
    await this.notificationService.notify(customer.id, 'PROFILE_UPDATED', customer.email);
    return this.customerRepository.update({ where: { id }, values });
  }
}

@expected customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';
import { NotificationService } from '../../infrastructure/services/notification.service';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(NotificationService) protected notificationService: NotificationService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }
    return customer;
  }

  public async update(id: string, values: Partial<Customer>) {
    const customer = await this.findOne(id);
    await this.notificationService.notify(customer.id, 'PROFILE_UPDATED');
    return this.customerRepository.update({ where: { id }, values });
  }
}

@start customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
  lastExportedAt?: Date;
  marketingOptIn: boolean;
};

@expected customer.entity.ts
export type Customer = {
  id: string;
  email: string;
  name: string;
  marketingOptIn: boolean;
};

@start notification.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class NotificationService {
  public async notify(customerId: string, event: string) {
    return this.transport.send(customerId, event);
  }
}
